## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of materials with metal ions as nodes and organic ligands as linkers, 1  which could form three dimensional frameworks with unique properties containing high pore volume, large surface area and extraordinary thermal stability.2 With these excellent properties, MOFs have been applied in gas storage, separation, drug delivery and chemical sensing.3-6 Among them, Cu-MOFs have received special concerns in electrochemical applications because of their well-defined configuration, unique electric conductivity, and superior catalytic activity.7-9 For instance, morphology- and size-controlled nanoscale Cu-MOFs were prepared by Pang’s group using benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate as organic ligand.10 The porous carbon functionalized Cu-MOFs with large pore volume and surface area were
## Analytical Chemistry
reported by Chen’s group as the matrix of the electrolyte for the construction of electrochemical capacitor.11 Michael et al synthesized a 2D chemical structure of CuMOFs to apply in chemiresistive detection based on the high electrical conductivity, which exhibited excellent electrochemical response.12  Furthermore, Cu-MOFs were reported to construct biosensor based on their catalytic activity for the detection of small molecules such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , NADH, AA and glucose.13-15 Inspired by these unique properties of Cu-MOFs, Au nanoparticles functionalized Cu-MOFs (AuNPs/CuMOFs) were synthesized in this work, which were not only used as nanocarriers to immobilize a large amount of hairpin probes 3 (HP3) and as electroactive materials for signal report, but also acted as a catalyst to oxide glucose for enzyme-free signal amplification.
Lipopolysaccharide (LPS), often known as endotoxin, is a major ingredient of the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria and composed by three distinct regions: O-specific antigen, lipid A and core polysaccharide.16-18 Since LPS is responsible for microcirculation disturbance, fever reaction, diarrhoea, septic shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation,19-20 the detection of LPS is urgently required in medical, therapeutics and food security. Although a variety of LPS immunosensors have been developed based on the specific recognition between LPS and LPS binding antibodies,21-22 the process of extracting antibodies from animal body is complex and time-consuming, and it is difficult to guarantee the same batch-to-batch quality of antibodies.23-24 Fortunately, since the highest affinity aptamer to LPS was selected by $\mathrm{Kim}^{25}$ et al in 2012, it provided a more optional platform to construct aptasensors for LPS detection because aptamer exhibited higher specificity and affinity towards the target compared with antibodies.26-27 Cho et al described a label free electrochemical LPS aptasensor by immobilizing LPS aptamer directly on a 3-mercaptopropinic acid
## Analytical Chemistry
modified gold electrode. 28  In our previous work, a signal-on electrochemical aptasensor for LPS was reported based on a three-way DNA junction-aided recycling,29 which showed good selectivity and wide linear range. However, owing to the ultralow levels of LPS in serum, attempts could still be made to improve the sensitivity by multiple signal amplification strategies. Recently, due to the high efficiency and adaptability, target-induced cyclic amplification strategies were widely applied in nucleic acid detection.30-31 Especially the quadratic cyclic amplification has attracted more attention because of the powerful amplifying effect and higher sensitivity.32-33 However, quadratic cyclic amplification almost depends on the DNA signal output, thus, it has rarely been applied to protein detection. In this work, by converting the LPS content into DNA signal output, we introduced quadratic amplification of target cyclic-induced polymerization and nicking endonuclease (N.BstNBI)-mediated cyclic hairpin assembly for LPS detection using electrochemical method with high sensitivity.
Herein, based on enzyme-free catalysis amplification of Cu-MOFs and targettriggered quadratic cycles, a ratiometric electrochemical aptasensor for LPS was reported with high sensitivity and accuracy. As shown in Scheme 1(A), the target cyclic-induced polymerization was performed as follows. Firstly, hairpin probes 1 (HP1) contained three domains: sequence 1 of LPS aptamer (the red part in Scheme 1 (A)), sequence 2 of partially complementary to a primer chain (the blue part in Scheme 1(A)) and sequence 3 of an exposed single strand of HP1 stems (the cyan part in Scheme 1(A)). In the presence of target LPS, sequence 1 would specifically combine with the LPS to form G-tetrad structure, and then sequence 2 was exposed to hybridize with the primer. Next, with the aid of phi29, the complementary pair extended from $3^{\prime}$ to $5^{\prime}$ of HP1, leading the target LPS to drop from HP1 and take part
## Analytical Chemistry
in the next cycle (cycle I). After that, a large amount of output DNA (a partially complementary DNA duplex) were produced to take part in the N.BstNBI-mediated cyclic hairpin assembly. The process details were showed as follows, the sequence 2 of the output DNA were hybridized with the ferrocene (Fc) labeled hairpin probes 2 (Fc-HP2) firstly, and then in the presence of N.BstNBI, output DNA were released from the hybridized structure to initiate the next hybridization process (cycle II). Simultaneously, the cleavage sites of Fc-HP2 were cut and a large amount of singlestranded capture probes were produced, and then capture probes could be hybridized with Au nanoparticles/Cu-MOFs labeled hairpin probes 3 (HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs). After the quadratic cycles and the hybridization between capture probes and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs, the original signal molecules of Fc left from the electrode surface while Cu-MOFs were brought to electrode surface, which caused the signal increasing of Cu-MOFs and the signal decreasing of Fc for ratiometric readout. With glucose in the detection solution, Cu-MOFs could catalyze the oxidation of glucose to realize the enzyme-free signal amplification. By measuring the peak currents ratio of the Cu-MOFs and Fc $(I_{\mathrm{Cu-MOFs}}/I_{\mathrm{Fc}})$ , the proposed aptasensor for LPS detection exhibited a wide linear range from $1.0~\mathrm{fg/mL}$ to $100~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ and a low detection limit of $0.33\mathrm{fg/mL}$ with high sensitivity and accuracy.
