HP1 contains three domains: sequence 1 of LPS aptamer was expressed as bold, sequence 2 of partially complementary to primer was expressed as italics and sequence 3 of an exposed single strand of HP1 stems was expressed as single underline. Fc-HP2: the recognition sequence of N.BstNBI (GAGTC) was expressed as double underline. Tris-HCl buffer was used to prepare all oligonucleotides samples. And the oligonucleotides samples were heated to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and then cooled to room temperature for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ before use.
Apparatus and Characterization. A CHI 660D electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chenhua Instrument, China) was used to carry out electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), cyclic voltammetry (CV) and differential pulse
## Analytical Chemistry
voltammetry (DPV) measurements. A three-electrode system consisted of a platinum wire auxiliary electrode, a saturated calomel reference electrode (SCE) and the modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE) working electrode. S4800 scanning electron microscopy (Hitachi Co, Japan) was used for investigating the morphologies of various nanomaterials. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (Thermoelectricity Instruments, USA) was used to analyze the elemental composition of the synthesized nanomaterials.
The preparation of Au nanoflowers (AuNFs). The AuNFs were synthesized as follows. At first, $2~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.5\%$ nafion and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\lfloor\%\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ were added to $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ deionized water containing $20~\mathrm{mM}$ DA, and then the mixture solution was stirred at room temperature for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ until it turned light red. Finally, the resulted product was washed with deionized water and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for further use.
The synthesis of HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs. Cu-MOFs were prepared according to the literature34 with some revision. First, $0.20\mathrm{~g~}$ PVP was dissolved into a mixture which contained $4~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and $4~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol. Subsequently, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF containing $18.12~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and $5.43~\mathrm{mg}~2$ -amino terephthalic acid ( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC) were added into the above solution with ultrasonication for $15~\mathrm{min}$ . Next, the solution was heated in a Teflon-lined autoclave at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that $20~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF was added to dissolve the obtained precipitate, and then the solution was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{{h}}$ to remove the unreacted reagents. Finally, the obtained Cu-MOFs were collected by centrifugation and dispersed in $2\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water for further use.
AuNPs/Cu-MOFs were obtained by dropping $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $1\%\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ and $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of 2 mM $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ into the above Cu-MOFs solution and stirring for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ . After centrifugation and washed, AuNPs/Cu-MOFs were collected. Next, $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $2~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ HP3 was added in the AuNPs/Cu-MOFs solution by stirring for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then
## Analytical Chemistry
the HP3 labeled AuNPs/Cu-MOFs (HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs) was obtained by the strong interaction between the $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ groups of HP3 and AuNPs. The unconjugated HP3 were removed by centrifugation and washing, and then the obtained HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs were dispersed in $2\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for further use. The preparation procedure of HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs signal probes was shown in Scheme 1 (B).
Fabrication of the aptasensor. First, 0.3, $0.05~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ alumina were used respectively to polished the bare GCE $\langle\varPhi\ =\ 4\ \mathrm{{mm}}\rangle$ ) for obtaining a mirror-like electrode surface. Subsequently, $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ AuNFs was dropped onto the bare GCE to achieve AuNFs/GCE modified electrode. And then the modified electrode was incubated with $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Fc-HP2 $(10~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, $1.0~\mathrm{mM}$ HT as a blocking reagent was dropped the electrode surface for 50 min to eliminate nonspecific binding effects.
The recycle process. The recycle process of target cyclic-induced polymerization (cycle I) and N.BstNBI-mediated cyclic hairpin assembly (cycle II) was shown in Scheme 1 (A). The cycle I was performed as follows: $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ HP1, 10 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ primer and LPS with various concentrations were added to $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ phi29 buffer 1 $33~\mathrm{mM}$ Tris-acetate, $10\ {\mathrm{mM}}\ {\mathrm{Mg}}.$ -acetate, 66 mM K-acetate, 1 mM DTT, $\mathrm{pH}~7.5\$ ) containing phi29 $\mathrm{100~U/mL}$ ) and dNTPs $(200~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , and then the mixture solutions were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $80\mathrm{min}$ to obtain the output DNA.
The cycle II was performed as follows: $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the resultant output DNA solution was added into $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $1{\times}\mathrm{NEB}$ buffer 3 containing N.BstNBI $(100~\mathrm{U/mL})$ . Next, the $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ mixture solution was dropped onto the HT/Fc-HP2/AuNFs/GCE at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ . After that a large amount of capture probes were obtained.
## Analytical Chemistry
Measurement procedure. After the quadratic cycles were performed, the electrode was rinsed with deionized water to remove the unbound reagents, and then $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs was dropped into the electrode for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . DPV measurement was used to carry out the detection experiment. And the DPV parameters were a sweeping rate $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ , $50~\mathrm{ms}$ pulse width, 0.2 s pulse period and voltage range from -0.4 to $0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ . The comparative DPV responses of the prepared electrode with and without target LPS were measured and shown in Scheme 1(A).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characteristics of the different nanomaterials. The morphologies of the synthesized different nanomaterials were characterized using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). In Fig. 1A, it could be seen that the Au nanoparticles showed flowers-like shape so that we named them as Au nanoflowers (AuNFs), which might be attributed to that the nafion (a kind of cationic polymer with sulfonate groups onto a tetrafluoroethylene backbone) acted as a template for $\mathrm{\AuCl_{4}}^{-}$ reduction and nucleation to form nanoflowers-like structure. Fig. 1B and Fig. 1C exhibited the SEM images of Cu-MOFs and AuNPs/Cu-MOFs, respectively. It was shown that the particle size of Cu-MOFs was $200{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and Cu-MOFs displayed well-formed spheres which consisted of single-layer nanosheets with large surface area. In addition, the background of Cu-MOFs was clear without other impurities, suggesting that CuMOFs owned stable structure which was suitable for aptasensor construction. With AuNPs produced, it could be seen that Cu-MOFs were surrounded by many light nanoparticles which was assigned to AuNPs, illustrating AuNPs were almost adsorbed to the external of Cu-MOFs by chemical adsorption between AuNPs and - $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ of Cu-MOFs.
## Analytical Chemistry
In addition, the analysis of elemental composition of AuNPs/Cu-MOFs was also characterized with X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Fig. 1D (a), the characteristic peaks of Au4f, C1s, N1s, O1s, and $\mathtt{C u2p}$ were obviously observed in the obtained AuNPs/Cu-MOFs XPS spectrum. And then Fig. 1D (b) showed the XPS doublet of Au4f ( $84.2\mathrm{eV}$ and $87.9\mathrm{eV}$ for the $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ ), which indicated the formation of AuNPs.35-36 Besides, the peaks at 285.1 eV, 399.4 eV and $531.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ belonged to C1s, N1s and O1s respectively (Fig. 1D (c)–(e)). Moreover, in Fig. 1D (f), the peaks at $934.4~\mathrm{eV}$ and $954.0~\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of ${\mathrm{CuO}},{^3}$ 7 and the peaks located at $943.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $962.4~\mathrm{eV}$ were ascribed to the open $3\mathrm{d}^{9}$ shell of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .38 No peaks of zero-valent and monovalent Cu was observed, which could be inferred that divalent Cu ions were acted as nodes to combine with organic ligands forming Cu-MOFs. Thus, the XPS results should give the proof to the characterization of the elemental composition of AuNPs/Cu-MOFs.
## Analytical Chemistry
Electrochemical characterization of different modified electrodes. The stepwise fabrication of the aptasensor was characterized by CV and EIS. Fig. 2A showed the CVs of the electrode modification in $20~\mathrm{mM}$ Tris-HCl $\mathrm{(pH}\ 7.5)$ contained $5.0\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4-}}$ at a scan rate of $100\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ from $-0.2$ to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ . As shown in Fig. 2A, the bare GCE exhibited a pair of well-defined redox peak (curve a). With AuNFs dropped onto electrode, the redox peak current (curve $b$ ) was increased since AuNFs could provide a larger electrochemical surface area and accelerate the electron transfer. Subsequently, with the assembly of Fc-HP2 (curve $c$ ), a decreased redox peak current was achieved due to the repulsion effect between negatively-charged FcHP2 and negatively-charged $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ . After the modified electrode was blocked with HT, a further decrease of the redox peak current was observed (curve d). Finally, the peak current increased apparently (curve $e$ ) after the modified electrode was incubated with $1{\times}\mathrm{NEB}$ buffer 3 containing N.BstNBI ( $100~\mathrm{U/mL}$ ) and output DNA, which was attributed to that the cleavage sites of Fc-HP2 were cut and capture probes were produced with the aid of N.BstNBI, and then the repulsion effect reduced because the negative charges of capture probes were less than those of Fc-HP2.
EIS was also commonly used to study the interface properties of the modified electrode surfaces. Generally, the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{et}})$ corresponded to the semicircle diameter at higher parts of the Nyquist plot, and the varies of $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ were strongly related to different electrode modification. Fig. 2B illustrated the EIS of the modified electrode in Tris-HCl contained $5.0~\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-4-}$ within the frequency range of $10^{-2}$ to ${10^{6}}\mathrm{Hz}$ at the potential of $220~\mathrm{mV}$ . The bare GCE displayed a small semicircle (curve $a$ ). After AuNFs were dropped on the electrode, $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ decreased significantly (curve $b$ ) owing to the conductive property of AuNFs. And then an increased $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ was acquired (curve $c$ ) when Fc-HP2 were immobilized onto the
## Analytical Chemistry
AuNFs/GCE. Moreover, the $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ kept increasing after the the modified electrode incubating with HT (curve $d$ ), which was attributed to the fact that HT bounded the interfacial electron transfer. After single-stranded capture probes were obtained with the assist of N.BstNBI, it could be seen that the $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ decreased (curve $e$ ) because the coulomb force between the capture probes and $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-4-}$ became weaker than that between Fc-HP2 and $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ .
Feasibility Study. In order to verify the feasibility of the designed quadratic cycles for LPS detection, DPV obtained in a series of control experiments were depicted in Fig. 3. First, the original DPV response (curve $a$ ) was obtained by HT/FcHP2/AuNFs/GCE in detection buffer of Tris-HCl $\mathrm{\pH\}7.5)\$ . As seen from curve $a$ , only the peak current of Fc was observed at 0.16 V. Next, HT/Fc-HP2/AuNFs/GCE was incubated with the reaction mixture (contained target LPS, phi29, N.BstNBI), and then incubated with HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs and tested in detection buffer of $\mathrm{pH}~7.5$ Tris-HCl. It could be seen that a strong DPV response of Cu-MOFs was appeared at - 0.18 V and a decreased DPV response of Fc was obtained at 0.16 V (curve $b$ ), which demonstrated that the cleavage sites of Fc-HP2 were cut and capture probes were
## Analytical Chemistry
obtained. As a result, Fc molecules left from the electrode surface and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs were approached to the electrode surface by the hybridization with capture probes. Furthermore, when $5.0\mathrm{mM}$ glucose was added into the detection buffer, the curve $c$ was achieved by incubating HT/Fc-HP2/AuNFs/GCE with the reaction mixture and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs. It could be found the peak current of Cu-MOFs increased while the peak current of Fc kept almost unchanged compared with curve $b$ , illustrating that Cu-MOFs exhibited good catalytic activity to the glucose oxidation. And according to the previous work, 39  the possible catalytic reaction of glucose oxidation by Cu-MOFs might be as follows: Cu (II)-MOFs was firstly oxidized to Cu (III)-MOFs in higher potential, and then Cu (III)-MOFs could oxidize glucose to gluconolactone, resulting in the regeneration of Cu (II)-MOFs. Simultaneously, the catalysis of Cu-MOFs to the glucose oxidation had no effect on the signal of Fc.
Additionally, control experiments were carried out with no target LPS (curve $d$ ), no phi29 (curve e), no N.BstNBI (curve $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{f}})$ , respectively. When target LPS was absent in the reaction mixture (curve $d$ ), the peak current of Fc at $0.16\mathrm{~V~}$ kept almost unchanged compared with curve a. It was due to the fact that the conformation of HP1 kept unchanged without target LPS, and then there was no output DNA to hybridize with Fc-HP2 for producing the cleavage sites, thus, the peak current of Fc kept unchanged. Moreover, a small peak current of Cu-MOFs at $-0.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ was appeared owing to the physical adsorption.
When phi29 was absent in the reaction mixture (curve $e$ ), the peak current of Fc at $0.16\mathrm{~V~}$ decreased and a larger peak current of Cu-MOFs at $-0.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ was observed compared with curve a. It was attributed to that LPS combined with HP1 triggering the expose of single strands which could hybridize with Fc-HP2, and then the
## Analytical Chemistry
cleavage sites of Fc-HP2 could be cut, which lead Fc to leave from the electrode and capture probes were obtained to hybridize with HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs signal probes. However, owing to the absence of phi29, LPS could not be dropped from HP1 to take part in cycle I for obtaining output DNA, so the cleavage degree of Fc-HP2 was limited. Compared with the quadratic cycles (curve $c$ ), the peak current changes of both Fc and Cu-MOFs were smaller as only cycle I was realized.
When N.BstNBI was absent in the reaction mixture (curve $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{f}})$ , the peak current of Fc at 0.16 V decreased a little compared with curve $a$ and a small peak current of CuMOFs at -0.18 V was obtained, which was ascribed to the fact that output DNA could be obtained to hybridize with Fc-HP2 forming hybridized structure, and the distance between the hybridized structure and the electrode surface was farther compared with that between Fc-HP2 and the electrode surface. While the cleavage sites of Fc-HP2 couldn’t be cut to obtain capture probes without the assist of N.BstNBI, thus, HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs couldn’t be introduced onto the electrode surface. So that the peak current of Fc decreased a little compared with curve a. In addition, the small peak current of Cu-MOFs was observed due to the physical adsorption.
