The optimized conditions of the aptasensor. In order to achieve optimal sensing performance, the important experimental parameters such as the concentration of glucose, the reaction time for cycle I and cycle II, and the hybridization time between capture probes and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs to the response of aptasensor were investigated by the aptasensor with $1.0~\mathrm{\mathrm{ng/mL}}$ LPS as a model. The signal of Cu-MOFs was directly related to the concentration of glucose, thus, the concentration of glucose in $\mathrm{pH}7.5$ Tris-HCl buffer was evaluated from 0 to $7.0~\mathrm{mM}$ and the other conditions were showed as follows (reaction time for cycle I of $100\mathrm{min}$ , reaction time for cycle II of $60~\mathrm{\min}$ , and hybridization time between capture probes and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs of $120~\mathrm{min}$ ). As shown in Fig. 4A, the catalysis peak current of Cu-MOFs increased with the increase of glucose concentration from 0 to $5.0~\mathrm{mM}$ , after that the catalysis peak current kept constant. Besides, the peak current of Fc remained almost unchanged with the increasing of glucose concentration, which was attributed to that the cleavage degree of Fc-HP2 achieved to a stable state with reaction time for cycle I and cycle II keeping unchanged. Thus, $5.0\mathrm{mM}$ was chose as the optimized concentration of glucose.
The reaction time for cycle I (assisted by phi29) was studied from 20 to $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and the other conditions were fixed as follows (the reaction time for cycle II of $60\mathrm{min}$ , the hybridization time between capture probes and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs of $120~\mathrm{min}$ ,
