The reaction time for cycle II (assisted by N.BstNBI) was also studied by ranging it from 10 to $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the other conditions were kept as follows (the reaction time for cycle I of $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the hybridization time between capture probes and HP3/AuNPs/Cu-MOFs of $120\mathrm{min}$ , and the glucose concentration in $\mathrm{pH}7.5$ TrisHCl buffer of $5.0~\mathrm{mM}$ ). It can be seen in Fig. 4C, as the reaction time increased, the catalysis peak current of Cu-MOFs increased and the peak current of Fc decreased. Since more Fc-HP2 were cut with the aid of N.BstNBI for the signal decrease of Fc, and then more capture probes were produced to hybridize with more HP3/AuNPs/CuMOFs for the signal increase of Cu-MOFs. When the reaction time went to $50~\mathrm{min}$ , the stable peak current change trends of Cu-MOFs and Fc were obtained. Thus, we chose 50 min as the optimized reaction time.
